Context: the 'Teichmülleries' notes on the genus-0/genus-1 base of the Teichmüller tower. This page compares the fundamental group of the projective line minus {0,1,∞} (the Legendre λ-line, i.e. the modular curve of level 2) with the modular groups SL_2 and GL_2.

The groups. Π_0 = ⟨λ_0, λ_1, λ_∞ | λ_∞λ_1λ_0 = 1⟩ is a free group (discrete or profinite) on two generators, the π_1 of P^1−{0,1,∞}. τ is complex conjugation, giving Π'_0 = {1,τ}·Π_0, the 'real' fundamental group. μ = {±1}, and Π = Π_0 × μ.

Identifications with modular groups. Π ≅ 𝔖^{+!} and Π'_0 ≅ 𝔖^!_0. The map Π_0 → 𝔖 with λ_i ↦ −λ_i shows that 𝔖 contains −1 centrally. This strongly suggests:
- 𝔖 = GL_2(Z) and 𝔖^+ = SL_2(Z), possibly in the guise of (quadrangular) cartographic groups;
- '!' means level-2 rigidification, so 𝔖^{+!} = Γ(2) = {±1} × F_2 and 𝔖^! = Γ(2) in GL_2(Z) = Π'_0 × μ;
- the index-6 arrows reflect GL_2(F_2) = SL_2(F_2) = S_3 permuting {0,1,∞};
- 𝔖^+ ⊂ 𝔖 has index 2 (determinant ±1).

The 'cube' (II). It assembles the index-2 extensions by τ and by ±1: μ_τ → D_τ = μ_τ × μ, Π'_0 → Π', Π_0 → Π, with every edge of index 2. A second cube places the M-groups above the 𝔖-groups: M^! → M and M^{+!} → M^+ have index 6, and M^+ ⊂ M has quotient Ẑ^*. So M, M^+ are 'arithmetic' enlargements; the most plausible reading is GL_2(Ẑ) ⊃ SL_2(Ẑ), or a cyclotomic image of the arithmetic π_1 of M_{1,1}. The determinant/cyclotomic character plays the role that ±1 plays for 𝔖.

The quotient remark. A marginal note says 𝔖/Π_0 and 𝔖^+/Π_0 are obtained by imposing relations τ'(ρ) = τ(ρ), or τ(φ) = ρ. Grothendieck adds the warning 'NB τ(φ) = (ℓ_i^{-1})ρ !' (a twist by inertia generators).

Section III, 'M(0) et groupes d'inertie'. This is a cube of cusp-stabilizer data at the point 0:
- L^Π_0 ≅ Ẑ is the inertia at 0 in Π (unipotent cusp subgroup);
- D^Π_0 = L^Π_0 ⋊ μ_τ is the decomposition group in Π';
- M^Π[0] is its normaliser/decomposition group in M^!;
- M(0) is a complement playing the role of μ_τ at the arithmetic level (torus/Galois part at a tangential base point at 0);
- the 𝔖-level analogues L^𝔖_0 ⊂ D^𝔖_0 ⊂ M^𝔖[0] receive these.
L^Π_0 ⊂ L^𝔖_0 has index 2: ⟨[[1,2],[0,1]]⟩ inside ⟨[[1,1],[0,1]]⟩, which is correct. A pencilled L^{Π_0}_0 at the bottom starts the version without ±1.

Purpose: to set up the dictionary between the Galois/inertia structure of π_1(P^1−{0,1,∞}) and that of the modular tower (M_{1,1} with level 2) in a form adapted to cusps and tangential base points.
